# Cloudflare Pages headers for Open Fly.
#
# Nothing here is content-hashed (OpenDoctrinesAgent.wasm is always called that,
# and a new game release replaces it in place), so every load revalidates. An
# unchanged file answers 304 with no body; a new release arrives at once.
#
# No X-Frame-Options and no frame-ancestors: Open Fly is meant to be framed, by
# the Open Doctrines website, by itch.io, and by anyone who wants to embed it.
/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=(), midi=()

/*.wasm
  Content-Type: application/wasm
/*.mjs
  Content-Type: text/javascript; charset=utf-8
/*.js
  Content-Type: text/javascript; charset=utf-8
# ── THE PART FILES' TYPE IS CHOSEN FOR COMPRESSION ──
#
# The parts are packed with --no-gzip, which is a correctness flag: a gzip
# stream cut into pieces has its header in .000 only, and itch.io re-encodes by
# content, so it inflated that one piece and passed the rest through and the
# page assembled 58,539,143 bytes of a 91,106,470-byte file. Plain parts have
# no such asymmetry -- see tools/pack_web.py.
#
# The saving was meant to come back as transit compression instead. It does not,
# unasked: `.part.000` is an extension no host knows, so these are served as
# application/octet-stream, and Cloudflare compresses by content-type with
# application/octet-stream not on its list. The same trap was measured in the
# Open Doctrines repository, where the .wasm came down brotli'd at 3.1 MB and
# the .data came down at its full 10 MB beside it.
#
# So the parts are declared application/wasm, which is on the compressible list.
# packed.js fetches them as an ArrayBuffer and never reads the type, and nothing
# hands them to instantiateStreaming, so the declaration costs nothing. The
# connectome gzips from 86.9 MB to 45.3 MB, which is the difference between
# 114 MB and 69 MB to reach the first turn.
#
# MEASURE IT, do not trust this comment: fetch a part from the deployed site
# and compare encodedBodySize with decodedBodySize. Cloudflare has its own
# opinions about compressing very large responses, and these parts are 20 MB.
/data/connectome.bin.part.*
  Content-Type: application/wasm
/agent/OpenDoctrinesAgent.data.part.*
  Content-Type: application/wasm

# The parts are fetched as bytes and joined by the page itself. A host is free
# to gzip them in transit -- the browser hands back exactly the bytes that were
# sent, and the join still reconstructs the file -- but they must never arrive
# with a Content-Encoding the page would have to undo twice.
/data/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
